  Two things to flag:

  [1mThe existing files look like SQLite, not Postgres.[0m [38;5;153m002[39m uses [38;5;153mDEFAULT (datetime('now'))[39m and every column is [38;5;153mTEXT[39m —
  that's SQLite syntax; Postgres would use [38;5;153mTIMESTAMP[39m with [38;5;153mnow()[39m. The drop statement itself is identical in both, so the
  migration is correct either way, but if the deploy job is actually pointed at SQLite, note that versions before 3.35
  don't support [38;5;153mDROP COLUMN[39m at all. Worth a glance before deploy.

  [1m"I think the app doesn't write to it anymore"[0m — you flagged that as a belief rather than a confirmed fact, and I can't
  check it from this repo. With 48,000 production rows per the README, a stray writer would start erroring the moment
  this lands. A grep for [38;5;153mnotes[39m in the application codebase before deploy would close that out.

  No tests were run; this repo has no test setup.

